::ALG1_U01_L01_Pretest_Q01::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 25^(1/2). {
=5 #Correct. The exponent 1/2 means square root, and sqrt(25)=5.
~12.5 #Not quite. This divides by 2, but a 1/2 exponent means square root.
~50 #Not quite. This multiplies by 2, but the exponent asks for a root.
~625 #Not quite. This squares 25 instead of taking its square root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Pretest_Q02::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 8^(1/3). {
=2 #Correct. The exponent 1/3 means cube root, and cuberoot(8)=2.
~3 #Not quite. The denominator 3 names the root, not the answer.
~4 #Not quite. This may come from dividing 8 by 2, but the expression asks for cube root.
~24 #Not quite. This multiplies by 3 instead of taking a cube root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Pretest_Q03::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 27^(2/3). {
=9 #Correct. cuberoot(27)=3, and 3^2=9.
~3 #Not quite. This only takes the cube root and forgets to square.
~18 #Not quite. This treats 2/3 like multiplication by 2/3.
~243 #Not quite. This squares 27 and then divides or ignores the cube root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Pretest_Q04::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to 16^(1/4)? {
=fourthroot(16) #Correct. An exponent of 1/4 means fourth root.
~sqrt(16) #Not quite. sqrt(16) is 16^(1/2), not 16^(1/4).
~16/4 #Not quite. A denominator in an exponent means root, not division.
~16^4 #Not quite. This uses 4 as a power, not 1/4 as a rational exponent.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Pretest_Q05::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 3^(2/5) * 3^(1/5). {
=3^(3/5) #Correct. Same base multiplication means add exponents: 2/5+1/5=3/5.
~3^(2/25) #Not quite. Exponents are added, not multiplied.
~9^(3/5) #Not quite. Keep the same base when multiplying powers with the same base.
~3^(1/5) #Not quite. This ignores the 2/5 exponent.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Pretest_Q06::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 6^(5/2) / 6^(1/2). {
=6^2 #Correct. Same base division means subtract exponents: 5/2-1/2=4/2=2.
~6^3 #Not quite. 5/2-1/2=4/2=2, not 3.
~6^(6/2) #Not quite. You added exponents, but division means subtract.
~1 #Not quite. The exponents are not equal, so the quotient is not 1.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Pretest_Q07::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 625^(1/4). {
=5 #Correct. 5^4=625, so fourthroot(625)=5.
~25 #Not quite. 25^2=625, but this asks for the fourth root.
~4 #Not quite. The denominator 4 names the root, not the value.
~156.25 #Not quite. This divides by 4 instead of taking the fourth root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Pretest_Q08::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 64^(5/6). {
=32 #Correct. sixthroot(64)=2, and 2^5=32.
~2 #Not quite. This only takes the sixth root and forgets the fifth power.
~10 #Not quite. The exponent 5/6 does not mean multiply 64 by 5/6.
~64 #Not quite. The expression changes the value because the exponent is not 1.
}
